p.src:
For the idealized transition discussed in class, an initially unentangled electron state may be written as a superposition of singlet and triplet components, $\ket{\uparrow\downarrow}
=
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
\left(\ket{S}+\ket{T_0}\right).$ With no photon initially present, the state is $\ket{\Psi(0)}
=
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
\left(\ket{S}+\ket{T_0}\right)
\otimes\ket{0_\gamma}.$ The singlet component remains a singlet without emission, while the excited triplet component can relax to the singlet and emit a photon: $\begin{aligned}\ket{S}\ket{0_\gamma}
&\longrightarrow
\ket{S}\ket{0_\gamma}, \\
\ket{T_0}\ket{0_\gamma}
&\longrightarrow
\ket{S}\ket{1_\gamma}.\end{aligned}$ Linearity then gives $\ket{\Psi_{\mathrm{final}}}
=
\ket{S}\otimes
\frac{\ket{0_\gamma}+\ket{1_\gamma}}{\sqrt{2}}.$

