p.src:
Classically, one point in phase space specifies the state: $(x,p).$ Quantum mechanics asks a different first question: what orthonormal basis defines the state space? For each real position $x$, introduce a generalized eigenket $\ket{x}$. A general state can be expanded as $\ket{\Psi}
=
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
dx\,\psi(x)\ket{x},
\qquad
\psi(x)=\braket{x}{\Psi}.$

