p.src:
The Hamiltonian $H$ is Hermitian, represents energy, and generates unitary time evolution. The time-dependent Schrödinger equation is $i\hbar\frac{d}{dt}\ket{\Psi(t)}
=
H\ket{\Psi(t)}.$ In the position representation, $i\hbar\frac{\partial\psi(x,t)}{\partial t}
=
H\psi(x,t),$ where the right side means the chosen position-space representation of the Hamiltonian acting on the function.

